oncoanalyser -> cBioPortal
=========================

Nextflow pipeline that converts oncoanalyser WGS/WTS output and clinical
CSVs into cBioPortal-ready files.

  oncoanalyser: https://github.com/nf-core/oncoanalyser
  cBioPortal:   https://www.cbioportal.org/


Quick start
-----------

  # 1. Clone
  git clone https://github.com/CRCHUM-CITADEL/nextflow-cbioportal.git
  cd nextflow-cbioportal/oncoanalyser

  # 2. Load modules
  module load nextflow/25.10.2 apptainer/1.3.5 htslib/1.22.1

  # 3a. Run (genomic mode)
  nextflow run main.nf -profile slurm,apptainer \
      --mode genomic \
      --genomic_samplesheet samplesheet.csv

  # 3b. Run (clinical mode)
  nextflow run main.nf -profile slurm,apptainer \
      --mode clinical \
      --clinical_samplesheet clinical_samplesheet.csv

  # 3c. Run (both mode — genomic + clinical in one run)
  nextflow run main.nf -profile slurm,apptainer \
      --mode both \
      --genomic_samplesheet samplesheet.csv \
      --clinical_samplesheet clinical_samplesheet.csv

  In "both" mode, the genomic workflow runs first and produces a
  linking file that maps sample IDs to clinical patient IDs. The
  clinical workflow then runs automatically using that linking file,
  so you do not need to provide --id_linking_file separately.

Use -params-file params.yaml to pass all parameters via a YAML file
instead of the command line.


Generating a genomic samplesheet
---------------------------------

  For oncoanalyser WGS/WTS output directories:

    python3 bin/generate_samplesheet.py /path/to/oncoanalyser_output \
        -o samplesheet.csv

  Scans for *_WGTS/ folders and produces a CSV with columns:
  group, subject_id, sample_id, folder.

  For DRAGEN DNA/RNA output directories (dna/ + rna/ subfolders):

    python3 create_samplesheet.py --input_dir /path/to/dragen_output

  Produces samplesheet.csv with columns: group_id, subject_id,
  sample_id, sample_type, sequence_data, info, filepath.


Generating a clinical samplesheet
----------------------------------

  Use the helper script to generate the samplesheet automatically:

    python3 bin/generate_clinical_samplesheet.py /path/to/clinical_dir \
        -o clinical_samplesheet.csv \
        -d 2025-05-20 \
        --study_id MY_COHORT

  Scans the directory for recognised ARGO/ICGC-ARGO CSV files
  (donors.csv, primary_diagnoses.csv, etc.) and produces a samplesheet
  with one row per file. Unrecognised CSV files are ignored with a
  warning. Paths in the output are absolute by default; use
  --relative-to /path/to/projectDir to write relative paths instead.

  The clinical samplesheet is a CSV that lists one row per clinical
  data file. Each row points to a CSV in ARGO/ICGC-ARGO format.

  Columns:

    group_id         Study/cohort identifier (no spaces)
    filetype         Data type — one of the 10 supported types below
    filepath         Absolute or relative path to the clinical CSV file
    extraction_date  Date data was exported (YYYY-MM-DD)
    info             Optional free-text note (may be empty)

  Supported filetypes:

    donors                  Patient demographics (sex, vital status, DOB)
    primary_diagnoses        Cancer type, staging, site, date of diagnosis
    treatments               Treatment summary (type, intent, start/end)
    surgeries                Surgical treatment details
    systemic_therapies       Chemotherapy / immunotherapy details
    specimens                Tumour and normal specimen metadata
    radiations               Radiation therapy details
    follow_ups               Disease status and relapse at follow-up
    sample_registrations     Sample → donor linkage (required for both mode)
    biomarkers               Lab biomarker values (PSA, ER/PR/HER2, etc.)

  Clinical CSV format: ARGO/ICGC-ARGO. Dates are stored as JSON
  day/month intervals relative to diagnosis (day 0):
    {"day_interval": 76, "month_interval": 2}

  Example clinical samplesheet (clinical_samplesheet.csv):

    group_id,filetype,filepath,extraction_date,info
    COHORT1,donors,/data/clinical/donors.csv,2025-05-20,
    COHORT1,primary_diagnoses,/data/clinical/primary_diagnoses.csv,2025-05-20,
    COHORT1,treatments,/data/clinical/treatments.csv,2025-05-20,
    COHORT1,surgeries,/data/clinical/surgeries.csv,2025-05-20,
    COHORT1,systemic_therapies,/data/clinical/systemic_therapies.csv,2025-05-20,
    COHORT1,specimens,/data/clinical/specimens.csv,2025-05-20,
    COHORT1,radiations,/data/clinical/radiations.csv,2025-05-20,
    COHORT1,follow_ups,/data/clinical/follow_ups.csv,2025-05-20,
    COHORT1,sample_registrations,/data/clinical/sample_registrations.csv,2025-05-20,
    COHORT1,biomarkers,/data/clinical/biomarkers.csv,2025-05-20,

  All 10 filetypes are optional. Include only the files you have.
  The minimum useful set is: donors, primary_diagnoses,
  sample_registrations.

  See assets/test_data/clinical/ for example CSVs for each filetype.


"Both" mode — genomic + clinical in one run
---------------------------------------------

  When --mode both is set, the genomic and clinical workflows run
  together in a single pipeline invocation. The key behaviour:

  1. The genomic workflow runs first and generates a linking file
     (a TSV mapping each subject_id to its sample_id). This linking
     file is produced automatically from the genomic samplesheet.

  2. The clinical workflow receives that linking file and uses it to
     filter clinical output to only the subjects present in the
     genomic data. You do NOT need to pass --sample_registrations
     separately.

  3. If --clinical_samplesheet is provided, the full clinical
     processing runs (patient + sample attribute files).

  4. If --clinical_samplesheet is omitted in both mode, the pipeline
     falls back to template mode: it generates a minimal
     data_clinical_sample.txt from the genomic linking file alone,
     with patient and sample IDs derived from the samplesheet.

  Example command:

    nextflow run main.nf \
        -profile slurm,apptainer \
        --mode both \
        --study_id "MY_STUDY" \
        --project_description "Description of my study" \
        --genomic_samplesheet samplesheet.csv \
        --clinical_samplesheet clinical_samplesheet.csv \
        --ensembl_annotations /path/to/biomart_grch38_ensembl_113.tsv \
        --ensembl_annotations_expr /path/to/biomart_grch38_ensembl_110.tsv \
        --genome_reference /path/to/Homo_sapiens_assembly38.fasta \
        --vep_data /path/to/vep_cache \
        --pcgr_data /path/to/pcgr_ref_data

  Both mode without clinical data (template output only):

    nextflow run main.nf \
        -profile slurm,apptainer \
        --mode both \
        --study_id "MY_STUDY" \
        --genomic_samplesheet samplesheet.csv \
        --ensembl_annotations /path/to/biomart_grch38_ensembl_113.tsv \
        --ensembl_annotations_expr /path/to/biomart_grch38_ensembl_110.tsv \
        --genome_reference /path/to/Homo_sapiens_assembly38.fasta


